  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nDiscussion:\n\nDry weather, along with more comfortable temps and dewpoints are \nexpected through Friday. This is due to our post-frontal airmass\nand the remaining deep trough in place across the eastern U.S.\nwhich continues to provide northerly flow across our region. \n\nFriday into Saturday, southerly flow returns as the East Coast \ntrough pushes further east and a new shortwave and trough emerge \nfrom out of the northern/central Great Plains. This southerly flow \nwill bring moisture back into the area. Increasing moisture combined \nwith incoming trough/low pressure and cold front will lead to \nchances for showers and storms returning to the area this weekend.\nSomething to keep an eye on will be the positioning and timing of\nthe low and cold front arrival. With it's approach, we do see an \nuptick in 850mb winds and a little bit of upper jet support. If \nthe front were to come through during peak heating, we certainly \ncould see a few strong storms as there will be at least some shear\npresent. Confidence is very low though as many things have to \ncome together for this to occur. Again, it's worth keeping an eye \non.\n\nThereafter, there are still model differences on whether or not this \nlow pressure system then becomes cut-off. If it does, then chances \nfor showers and storms will remain elevated into next week as this \nlow continues to slowly spins across the region with no where to \ngo. The previous Day 5, now day 4, ERO still mountains a \nslight/marginal risk for excessive rainfall across our area. \n\nBottom line, nice through Friday. Unsettled weather this weekend, \npotentially lasting into next week. Just beyond the end of this \nforecast period, it looks like the cut-off low loses it's circulation\nand becomes no more with high pressure then building back into \nthe region.",
